    "body": "One in four supply chain leaders wish they had made a different warehouse automation decision, according to a new Exotec report. But changing course could be costly, as 94% say switching providers would be prohibitively expensive.\nWhat’s Related\n“Avoid Robot Regret” surveyed 250 supply chain decision-makers in the U.S. and Canada . The warehouse robotics company found that disappointment often starts with decisions made before a system is installed, including how buyers calculate costs and assign responsibility when something goes wrong.\n“What we heard from supply chain leaders is that regret is rarely about the technology itself,” said Romain Moulin, CEO and co-founder of Exotec. “It starts when a system built by several vendors stops working as one, and the customer is left figuring out who is responsible for the fix. The operations that get the most from their automation are the ones where a single partner owns the whole system performance, from design through the years it runs.”\nLarger companies also reported problems with their automation investments. Among respondents at companies with more than $1 billion in revenue, 21% said their biggest automation investment fell short of expectations, compared with 13% at smaller companies.\nRELATED STORIES: The Skills Warehouse Companies Are Paying For Right Now | Robots for the Rest of Us: Warehouse Automation Gets Easier\nAutomation costs higher than expected\nNearly two-thirds of respondents, or 65%, said their total ownership costs were higher than expected. Yet 69% admitted they did not calculate the full lifetime cost before investing.\nThe report points to expenses that can be missing from an initial proposal, such as software and hardware upgrades or spare parts. Seven in 10 respondents said undisclosed automation costs had increased their annual maintenance expenses.\nOverruns were biggest among buyers whose systems fell short of expectations. Within that group, 43% said total costs were 26% to 100% higher than anticipated.\nWho fixes the system?\nMost respondents (60%) had automation designed by multiple companies. One in seven leaders said they had not seen the benefits of automation because no vendor took full responsibility for system performance.\nThe report urges buyers to establish who will handle problems before signing a contract. It also recommends asking what maintenance includes and how support will continue if the project manager leaves.\nPreparation was another trouble spot. More than a quarter of respondents, or 27%, said their warehouse wasn’t ready when they invested in automation.\nMethod Research prepared the online survey and RepData distributed it, with responses collected Aug. 17–31, 2026. Nearly every respondent, 249 of 250, had participated in an automation deployment at a current or previous employer.",
